\f0\i0 , Chanta muttered under his breath. Fortunately no one heard him; that was an argument even Chanta could figure out had passed beyond his winning.\
Awkward.\
Chanta was saved by the Chief of Staff of the High Elvis-elect circulating among the guests.\
\'93If everyone could take their places,\'94 the Chief of Staff said. \'93We\'92re about to begin.\'94\
Walter was still in Chanta\'92s throne, casting a territorial stare that said, 
\f2\i I\'92m the mayor of 
\fs20 GET BENT
\fs22 , and I don\'92t care if you are the Great Preacher or whatever they call you.
\f0\i0 \
Chanta waved the kid\'92s resting Fight Club face down. \'93Stay, there,\'94 he said, and sat on the edge of the dais. It was about as close to Sarah as he could get without his limbs turning to jack cheese, and it allowed his Elvisettes to huddle closer. Some sat on the dais; some sat on the floor at his feet. Tax! One removed his shoes and massaged his feet. He was glad he\'92d changed his socks that morning.\
The house lights went down and three spots focussed on the spa bath where a Clambake-series Elvis, predictably named Tom Wilson, climbed two wooden steps that had been screwed to the side of the spa bath. He stood on the mosaics, where the mermaids had been looking world-weary since before anybody in the room was born.\
\'93My fellow Elvi,\'94 Tom said. \'93Praise the Dawkins.\'94\

