\f1\fs24 \cf2 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f0\fs22 What followed was a celebration and a wake. The expressions on the faded portraits that hung from the faded wallpaper hardly changed their expressions at all\'97they\'92d seen more incongruous things before.\
Sarah found the comfiest couch in the room and flumped into it. She\'92d have preferred a King-size in one of the suites on the third floor, but devotees would have sought her out anyway. They came to her in ones and twos, then threes and fours. Before long they were a throng, and she could no longer see the faces of all of them. Mostly they just wanted to bless the One After the One, her Little Clinton. Some said, 
\f2\i Praise the Dawkins 
\f0\i0 and promised they\'92d do everything they could to stop the Holy Roller gospel nonsense. Some asked indiscreetly if Little Clinton was an immaculate conception. Others tried to conspiratorially confirm, \'93He\'92s the Great Teacher\'92s son, isn\'92t he? You can tell us, we won\'92t tell\'94.\
Way was made for The High Elvis, who joined Sarah on the couch. His Chief of Staff stood at the High Elvis\'92s side.\
There was a scuffle in the throng, and muted pleas of \'93Let me through, let me through.\'94 There were grumbles of wait your turn and threats of shooting someone\'92s television out. Then, slightly more distinctly, \'93I\'92m the mayor of 
\fs20 GET BENT
\fs22 .\'94\
Sarah recognised the voice. \'93Walter? Let him through.\'94\
The urchin mayor was pushed forward, and he almost lost his balance. Recovering, he stood head bowed while he tried to smooth out crumples in his rolled-up poster.\
The High Elvis ran a hand through his hair. It took effort. \'93Speak, Mayor of 
\fs20 GET BENT.\'94\

\fs22 Walter stammered. He held out the poster. \'93Could you autograph this please?\'94\
The High Elvis took it, looked at it for a moment, then took it and unrolled it. It was a Rock Against Revival bill, depicting head shots of the thirty original clone series (let no one speak of Johnny Tyronne). At the bottom, there was already a signature. The High Elvis looked at that for slightly longer.\
\'93Oh, son, you do me an honour,\'94 the High Elvis declared as he pulled out his parliamentary Sharpie and scrawled his 
\f2\i Tom Wilson 
\f0\i0 alongside the first, and handed it back unrolled to let the ink dry.\
\'93Thank you, sir.\'94 Delighted, Walter held the posted up to the light of a chandelier. He examined both autographs. They were identical. He rolled it back up without showing anyone, and went back to Chanta\'92s throne, where he watched the wake move beneath him and drank banana-based mocktails well into the night.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f0\fs22 They mingled together. Kathy held hands with Hayley and was happy, in part because she had not replaced her patch since they arrived. She liked the feeling of holding hands with Hayley and liked feeling the feeling. Kathy was a doctor attending a ceremony, a celebration and a wake, holding hands with Hayley who was an investigator attending a ceremony, a celebration and a wake. While they saw different things, what they saw together provided a picture no one else would see.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f0\fs22 For a time, they went up to the mezzanine and stood at the railing and Elvi-watched with holding-hands eyes, which were also doctor eyes and investigator eyes.\
Hayley noticed the swell and the flow of the crowd, the stationary clumps and the two\'92s and three\'92s driving among them, the singles lifting around, not finding a group they felt welcome in, the watchers from the edges, trying to look casual leaning against a wall or sitting the arm of a couch. There but not there. Among all that, a force moved and disrupted all the stable patterns wherever it went.\
Chanta moved about with relative ease, people making way for him and his entourage of Elvisettes like water off the bow of a ship, excited in his wake, shoe following fro a while, others finding themselves in new clumps either toasting the High Elvis or mourning this they had lost.\
Kathy noticed a Clambake with grey coming through his sideburns hauling himself, with effort, off a Chesterfield, his hand lingering its arms while he got steady on his feet. An Elvine came off a bar stool and stood a moment with her palm pressed flat into the small of her back. A Rusty Martin stood up from a low stool in one go, still talking, and went to the bar.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\fs22 \cf2 \u8220 ?Twenty-five years,\u8221 ? an Elvine said behind Kathy and Hayley. \u8220 ? years I\u8217 ?ve had that, and I went to a man in Bent Street about taking it off and he told me it\u8217 ?d cost more than the wedding did.\u8221 ?\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\fs22 \cf2 They turned round. A Maile Duval had her sleeve pushed up past the elbow and her arm out, and a small audience for it up there, and she was delighted with herself.\
\'93Let me,\'94 said Kathy.\
Maile put the arm into her hands with the satisfaction of a woman who has finally found somebody qualified to agree with her. It ran from the wrist to the inside of the elbow and it had been a serious thing once \'97 you could still see where it had pulled the skin either side of it out of true \'97 and now it was a pale suggestion you would have to be shown before you found it. The deep work down at the wrist had held on better than the rest, so what was left was the two ends of it with the middle gone out, like a word rubbed off a blackboard by somebody in a hurry.\
\'93When did it start going?\'94\
\'93That\'92s what I can\'92t tell you. I\'92ve looked at that every morning of my life, and then one morning I\'92m at the sink and I think, hang on.\'94 She turned the wrist over. \'93Best thing that\'92s happened to me all year, and I\'92m counting walking out of Bent Street.\'94\
Kathy turned the arm into the light of the nearest chandelier and did not let go of it.\
\'93Give me the other one.\'94\
\'93What?\'94\
\'93The hand, Maile. The one I sewed up.\'94\
Maile handed it over the way you hand a doctor a thing you have stopped thinking about, and Kathy turned it palm down and went across the back of it with her thumb, over a place she had put together herself on a night neither of them had ever mentioned since, and there was nothing there. No line, no silver, no ridge under the thumb. Nothing to find at all.\
Maile stopped enjoying herself. She took her hand back and held it up close and turned it over twice, and looked at it for a while with a different face on.\
\'93Huh,\'94 she said.\
Kathy gave the arm back, gently, and was already looking past her, because it was not the same on all of them. The Elvine beside Maile still had every mark she had walked in with. Dianne Carter, two along, had gone through a windscreen before Kathy ever knew her and you would not have known it tonight. Some of them were further into whatever this was than the others, and there was nothing in a single face in that group to tell you which.\
Dianne Carter had been watching her do it.\
\'93Go on then,\'94 she said.\
\'93I\'92m not doing anything.\'94\
\'93You\'92re doing something.\'94 She was not shouting, and it was worse than shouting. \'93Do you know what I\'92ve been doing since Bent Street? Going to funerals in perfect health. I\'92ve been the healthiest thing at [ ] of them. Cousins of mine are in the ground and I have never felt better in my life and nobody will tell me why, and now here\'92s Aunt Kathy going up and down the room looking at everybody\'92s arms.\'94 She held her own out, palm up. \'93So go on. Is it us? Are we next?\'94\
Kathy had never in her life pretended to an answer she did not have, so she took the arm and looked at it properly, and what she found in the crook of it was skin like the inside of a shell.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\fs22 \cf2 Below them the crowd opened again, and the Great Teacher came through it towards the curtains with two beers he had not asked for and his wake behind him, and stopped in front of the group down there, and looked at all their faces, and reached for something consoling.\
\
\'93You\'92re all looking well,\'94 said Chanta.\
It went down like a dropped tray.\
Kathy watched him try to get out from under it, and fail, and retreat into the beer, and take his wake back across the floor towards the dais.\
\
\
\'93Those Elvines you were talking to,\'94 said Kathy. \'93You know them?\'94\
\'93Sure. I know a lot of Elvines, Kathy.\'94\
\'93They\'92re looking well.\'94\
Chanta nodded. \'93I guess I just make them happy. They say I make them laugh.\'94\
Kathy had to credit that. People did tend to laugh around Chanta. \'93How many of them have you laughed all the way into bed?\'94\
\'93Kathy, are you jealous?\'94\
Even Hayley laughed at that.\
\'93Hey.\'94 Chanta was offended. \'93I\'92m the Great Teacher and possibly the richest cleaner in the history of the world.\'94\
\'93Your old friends liked you better when you were neither.\'94 Kathy sat down on the dais beside him, which nobody did, and which put her at the height of his ear. \'93Is it all of them? Have you slept with all of them?\'94\
Chanta looked at the group by the curtains. He looked at his own feet, which were being attended to. He was constitutionally unable to leave a silence alone, and a corner of him had spent his whole life waiting for somebody in authority to ask him a question he could actually answer.\
\'93All of them, if you want the truth. Every one of them.\'94 He drank some of the beer. \'93I\'92m not proud of it. Well. I\'92m a bit proud of it.\'94\
\'93Right,\'94 said Kathy. \'93Then you\'92re carrying something.\'94\
There was a pause of the kind that gets described in Parliamentary records as the House being briefly unable to proceed.\
Chanta went the colour of the curtains.\
\'93Now hang on \'97\'94\
\'93Chanta.\'94\
\'93I\'92ve never had anything.\'94 He had lowered his voice to a register that carried twice as far. \'93Not once. Not ever. There was one time in Tupelo Street but that was a rash off a wetsuit and the bloke said so in writing.\'94\
\'93That is not what I said.\'94\
\'93You said I\'92m carrying something. That\'92s what people mean when they say \'97\'94\
\'93I know what people mean.\'94 Kathy took the beer out of his hand, drank some of it, and gave it back. \'93Look at them. Not at me. At them.\'94\
Chanta looked.\
\'93Betty Lee,\'94 said Kathy. \'93Her hair\'92s gone back to the colour it was. Not dyed back. Gone back, at the roots, on its own. Marguerita\'92s been telling me about that knee since [ ] and last week she did the splits at a party and hasn\'92t shut up about it. Dianne Carter over there was on that plaza and has been to more funerals than I have and she has the arms of a girl.\'94 She let that sit. \'93And you. You were a mess when they brought you in and you have got steadily better ever since, in a way that nobody in this room has once remarked on, including you.\'94\
\'93I\'92ve been eating better \'97\'94\
\'93Chanta, I have been doctor to you people for a long time. I know what\'92s in an Elvis before I put a hand on one. The heart goes in the forties. The gut doesn\'92t work and never has. The knees, the pressure, the eyes. Half my practice is telling an Elvis in his prime that he is not going to see fifty, and the other half is burying the ones who didn\'92t believe me.\'94 She gestured at the room with the flat of her hand, at the whole crowded shabby length of it. \'93Look at this lot. There isn\'92t a bad knee in that group by the curtains. Nobody is holding a table to stand up. Nobody is out of breath and every single one of them was on that plaza when the ones we buried on Thursday were standing next to them.\'94\
\'93So they\'92re lucky.\'94\
\'93They are not lucky. Lucky is one. This is a set.\'94 Kathy put her hands flat on her knees. \'93Whatever\'92s in them isn\'92t making them ill. It\'92s doing the opposite. It\'92s not pathogenic, it\'92s salutogenic.\'94\
Chanta stared at her.\
\'93It\'92s a word,\'94 said Kathy.\
\'93It doesn\'92t sound like a word.\'94\
\'93It means it goes through you and tidies up.\'94\
\'93That\'92s not what that sounds like it means.\'94\
\'93No,\'94 Kathy agreed. \'93It isn\'92t.\'94\
From the chair, without turning round, Sarah said: \'93Then it can be moved.\'94\
She said it the way a person says something they have just this second thought of. She put no particular weight on it at all, and she was not looking at any of them. She was looking at Dianne Carter, who had her sleeve up and her chin up and had stood in the middle of a room full of people and demanded to be told what was wrong with her.\
Sarah had been eleven when she learned not to do that.\
\'93Then make some,\'94 said Chanta, to Kathy. \'93Whatever it is. Make it and hand it out. You\'92re a doctor. You\'92ve got a \'97 you\'92ve got machines.\'94\
\'93I have got machines,\'94 said Kathy. \'93I have got one machine, and it is very good, and if I start tonight and never sleep I can grow enough of that to save a handful of you by Christmas. A handful. That\'92s not medicine, that\'92s a hobby.\'94 She took her hand off her knee and pointed with two fingers at the room. \'93The thing that\'92s killing them does it in hours. I cannot outrun hours with a dish. Nobody could.\'94\
\'93So we\'92re stuffed.\'94\
\'93I didn\'92t say that.\'94\
\'93You said \'97\'94\
\'93I said I can\'92t make enough of it.\'94 Kathy looked at him. \'93It\'92s already being made. It has been getting made all year, in beds, all over this Ghetto, by you.\'94\
Chanta was not quick, and everybody present had arranged their expectations accordingly, and so nobody was watching him closely enough to see him do it.\
Walter, at a distance of one metre, was already grinning like a split melon.\
\'93Oh,\'94 said Chanta.\
\'93Chanta \'97\'94\
\'93No, hang on.\'94 He put the beer down on the boards without looking where. \'93Hang on.\'94\
\'93It is not that simple.\'94\
\'93It\'92s exactly that simple,\'94 said Chanta, with the terrible confidence of a man who has arrived at the correct answer by a route nobody else can follow. \'93You\'92ve just been saying it wrong.\'94\
He got up off the dais, and the crowd opened for him one more time, and this time it did not close behind him, because everybody in the room could see where he was going.\
He went up the two wooden steps onto the spa bath, in front of the mermaids, in the cape and the sash and the crown, and the Clambake turned over to look at him, because it always did.\
He did not do the ceremonial voice.\
\'93Brothers and sisters,\'94 said Chanta. \'93I\'92ve got good news.\'94\
They waited. They had waited exactly like this once before, in this room, for a man standing on this same bath, and that man had asked them to sweep the streets.\
\'93Nobody else has to die of this,\'94 he said. \'93That\'92s the first thing. The Holy Aunts have worked out what stops it and it\'92s already here, it\'92s in this room, and some of you have got it already and don\'92t know you have.\'94\
Nobody moved.\
\'93It\'92s rooting,\'94 said Chanta.\
The Clambake did not make a sound.\
\'93Sex,\'94 he said, in case the theological ones needed it spelled out. \'93It\'92s sex. That\'92s the whole of it. Whatever it is, it goes round the way that goes round, it\'92s been going round a while already, and every one of you it gets to is one it doesn\'92t get.\'94 He opened his arms, and the cape opened with him, and the light came off the rhinestones and went round the walls like something being handed out. \'93So there\'92s my first decree, and it\'92s the only one I\'92ve got. Go forth and hump.\'94
